@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@
+decision: local
+triggers: shared-cause, defect-type
+summary: All three r1 findings come from one decision, the column-backed `nudge_threshold` in Section 1 Storage/Registry, whose row-model semantics (absence = default, unset deletes the row, provenance columns on `seat_config`, strict registry range check) were never written for that key. One spec edit that defines the column-backed key contract closes all three, so no redesign is needed.
+pattern: one rule applied inconsistently. Section 1 specifies the generic `seat_config` row model, which covers `(default)` on get, `unset` = delete the row, `writer_provenance`/`writer_binding` and registry validation. It also says `nudge_threshold` is backed by `seats.nudge_threshold` (NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, gated by A5, parsed with a clamp by `NudgeThreshold::parse`), but never says how each row-model behaviour maps onto that column. r1 [Should-fix] Section 1 Storage ... `unset KEY` "delete the row" covers get/unset, r1 [Should-fix] Section 1 Storage (`seat_config` columns `writer_provenance`, `writer_binding`) ... covers provenance and the A5 operator exception, and r1 [Nit] Section 1 Registry ... covers validation versus the alias clamp. Each is one operation of the same unmapped contract. Keeping the column was a user decision (B10 "Key over existing column"), and the fix is one paragraph, so changing the backing would cost more than it saves.
+clusters:
+- column-backed-contract: r1 [Should-fix] Section 1 Storage ("A row exists only for a key set explicitly; absence means the registry default"; "`nudge_threshold` is a registry key whose backing store is the existing `seats.nudge_threshold` column") vs Commands (`get` "with `(default)` when unset"; `unset KEY` "delete the row"), r1 [Should-fix] Section 1 Storage (`seat_config` columns `writer_provenance`, `writer_binding`) vs Commands (`ht human config seat set [--seat S]` any key, any named local seat, "recorded as an operator decision with provenance `operator:local-user:<uid>`"); Trust policy section, r1 [Nit] Section 1 Registry (`nudge_threshold` integer -1..9; "an unknown key or out-of-range value is refused") vs Commands ("`nudge-threshold [N]` and `wake-on` stay as aliases … output and M12 are unchanged") | rule: in Section 1, add one "column-backed keys" paragraph that defines every generic config operation for a key whose backing is an existing column, and state the same for any future column-backed key. It covers: get (the `(default)` marker, either by equality with the default or by a marker row); unset (writes the registry default and resets the `wake_on` mirror); provenance (where it is recorded for column writes: a `seat_config` provenance row, columns on `seats`, or an explicit "none"); write authority (the A5 operator exception for `ht human config seat set --seat`, added to the Trust policy section and to TRUST-POLICY.md); and validation (where the alias clamp and the registry range check each apply, so `nudge-threshold -5` and `config seat set nudge_threshold -5` give a defined and documented result). Check that the registry's `backing` field description and Section 1's test list ("default vs explicit", "nudge_threshold backing") match the paragraph.
